# Starter rules for proving the AETHRA-SEC alert pipeline works.
#
# Each fires on a property of a real packet. Nothing here fabricates an alert:
# if none of these ever match, nothing is stored, and that is the correct
# outcome rather than a fault.
#
# These three are DELIBERATELY BROAD. They match ordinary traffic, which is
# what lets you confirm the plumbing in about a minute - run "ping 127.0.0.1"
# and the first rule fires - instead of waiting for a real attack to arrive.
#
# REPLACE THEM BEFORE YOU RELY ON THIS FOR MONITORING. Broad rules on a live
# network produce a constant stream of alerts, and a screen that always has
# alerts on it is a screen nobody reads. Snort's registered and community rule
# sets are at snort.org/downloads (a free account is needed for those, though
# not for Snort itself); unpack one into C:\Snort\rules and add an include line
# for it in aethra.conf.
#
# Local rule sids start at 1000000 by convention - that range is reserved for
# rules you write, so it cannot collide with a downloaded rule set.

alert icmp any any -> any any (msg:"ICMP echo request"; itype:8; sid:1000001; rev:1; classtype:misc-activity;)
alert tcp any any -> any any (msg:"TCP SYN to HTTP port"; flags:S; dsize:0; sid:1000002; rev:1; classtype:attempted-recon;)
alert udp any any -> any 53 (msg:"UDP traffic to DNS port"; sid:1000003; rev:1; classtype:misc-activity;)
